NNAAMMEE
     write - conduct interactive conversation with another user

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     wwrriittee _u_s_e_r [_t_t_y]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     wwrriittee opens a two-way conversation with another logged-in
     user.  It looks up _u_s_e_r in /eettcc/uuttmmpp, using _t_t_y to pick a
     particular login if that _u_s_e_r is logged in on more than one
     terminal, and opens the corresponding terminal for writing.
     It then prints UUsseerr uusseerr hhaass bbeeeenn nnoottiiffiieedd ...  on the
     caller's own terminal, and copies each character typed at
     its standard input to the recipient's terminal until end of
     file (the terminal's end-of-file character, if the input is
     a terminal) is reached.

     If the recipient's terminal denies writes (its execute per-
     mission bit for "other" is off) and the caller is the super-
     user, wwrriittee asks UUsseerr `uusseerr' iiss ddeennyyiinngg mmeessssaaggeess.  OOvveerrrriiddee?
     and proceeds only if the answer is yy; any other caller is
     simply refused.

     While the conversation is open, wwrriittee puts its own terminal
     into cbreak mode so it can watch for lines beginning with !.
     Such a line is not sent to the recipient; instead, the
     remainder of the line is read as a command and run with ssyyss-
     tteemm(), with its output appearing locally, after which the
     conversation resumes.  If both terminals are in "crt" mode
     (see ssttttyy), wwrriittee also echoes backspace characters specially
     so that character erase looks right at both ends.

     The conversation ends when the sender reaches end of file
     (interrupt, hangup, and quit are also caught and treated the
     same way); both terminals are restored to their previous
     modes, and wwrriittee sends the line EEOOTT to the recipient's ter-
     minal before exiting.  wwrriittee runs setuid to root so that it
     can test, and when permitted override, the destination ter-
     minal's write-permission bit; it drops this privilege with
     sseettuuiidd(ggeettuuiidd()) immediately after opening the destination
     terminal.

FFIILLEESS
          /eettcc/uuttmmpp       list of logged-in users
          /ddeevv/*          terminal special files

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     wwhhoo, wwaallll, mmssgg, mmeessgg

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     wwrriittee prints a diagnostic and exits with status 1 if _u_s_e_r is
     not logged in, is not logged in on the named _t_t_y, the des-
     tination terminal cannot be written to, or the recipient
     refuses the connection; a warning only (without exiting) is
     printed if the caller's own terminal cannot be replied to.


